<article class="env-lemma" id="0916">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0916">Lemma <span data-tag="0916">15.57.2</span>.</a> Let $R \to S$ be an epimorphism of rings. Let $I^\bullet $ be a complex of $S$-modules. If $I^\bullet $ is K-injective as a complex of $R$-modules, then $I^\bullet $ is a K-injective complex of $S$-modules. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      This is true because $\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _{K(R)}(N^\bullet , I^\bullet ) = \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _{K(S)}(N^\bullet , I^\bullet )$ for any complex of $S$-modules $N^\bullet $, see Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/08YS" data-tag="08YS">10.107.14</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>